1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,825
A welding torch with a blinding flame.

2
00:00:02,825 --> 00:00:05,691
A miner's lamp that runs
on a stone and water.

3
00:00:05,691 --> 00:00:07,733
Mangoes that ripen far too fast.

4
00:00:08,133 --> 00:00:09,152
Three puzzles.

5
00:00:09,152 --> 00:00:11,359
One gas hides behind all of them.

6
00:00:11,809 --> 00:00:13,580
Diya sees it in the kitchen.

7
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:17,843
Her grandmother opens a box of mangoes
and finds a small grey stone inside.

8
00:00:18,193 --> 00:00:20,255
Grandmother throws the stone away.

9
00:00:20,255 --> 00:00:22,629
It gives off a gas when it gets wet.

10
00:00:22,629 --> 00:00:24,756
Using it on fruit is not allowed.

11
00:00:25,256 --> 00:00:26,615
That gas is ethyne.

12
00:00:26,615 --> 00:00:31,153
By the end, you will know why it has
a triple bond, and why its hydrogen

13
00:00:31,153 --> 00:00:32,053
can leave.

14
00:00:32,624 --> 00:00:34,140
So what is an alkyne?

15
00:00:34,490 --> 00:00:39,375
An alkyne is a hydrocarbon, made
only of carbon and hydrogen, with one

16
00:00:39,375 --> 00:00:41,305
carbon carbon triple bond.

17
00:00:41,655 --> 00:00:45,113
Their general formula is
C n H two n minus two.

18
00:00:45,463 --> 00:00:48,405
The first one is ethyne, C two H two.

19
00:00:48,405 --> 00:00:50,392
Its old name is acetylene.

20
00:00:50,792 --> 00:00:53,398
Next comes propyne, C three H four.

21
00:00:53,798 --> 00:00:58,111
With four carbons, the triple bond
can sit at the end, in but one yne,

22
00:00:58,111 --> 00:01:00,364
or in the middle, in but two yne.

23
00:01:00,364 --> 00:01:02,034
These are position isomers.

24
00:01:02,684 --> 00:01:05,938
Let's build the triple
bond on our molecule bench.

25
00:01:06,288 --> 00:01:10,230
Each carbon in ethyne is s p hybridised:
two s p orbitals in a straight

26
00:01:10,230 --> 00:01:12,389
line, and two p orbitals left over.

27
00:01:12,789 --> 00:01:16,067
One s p orbital from each
carbon overlaps head on.

28
00:01:16,067 --> 00:01:18,279
That makes one strong sigma bond.

29
00:01:18,679 --> 00:01:20,939
The other s p orbitals hold the hydrogens.

30
00:01:20,939 --> 00:01:24,636
So the molecule is a straight line,
at one hundred and eighty degrees.

31
00:01:25,036 --> 00:01:26,913
Now the leftover p orbitals.

32
00:01:26,913 --> 00:01:29,434
The upright pair overlaps side by side.

33
00:01:29,434 --> 00:01:31,273
That is the first pi bond.

34
00:01:31,673 --> 00:01:35,036
The front and back pair
also overlaps side by side.

35
00:01:35,036 --> 00:01:38,915
That is the second pi bond,
at right angles to the first.

36
00:01:39,315 --> 00:01:42,392
The two pi clouds wrap
the carbons like a tube.

37
00:01:42,392 --> 00:01:45,267
One sigma plus two pi
makes the triple bond.

38
00:01:45,917 --> 00:01:50,127
Three bonds pull the carbons close:
one hundred and twenty picometres,

39
00:01:50,127 --> 00:01:53,468
against one hundred and
fifty four for a single bond.

40
00:01:53,868 --> 00:01:57,737
Its bond enthalpy is eight hundred
and twenty three kilojoules per mole.

41
00:01:58,137 --> 00:01:59,435
Now the surprise.

42
00:01:59,435 --> 00:02:03,200
The hydrogen on a triple bond
carbon is a little acidic.

43
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:05,087
It can leave as a proton.

44
00:02:05,487 --> 00:02:07,130
Think of a tug of war.

45
00:02:07,130 --> 00:02:11,740
An s p carbon has fifty percent s
character, so it holds the bond electrons

46
00:02:11,740 --> 00:02:12,922
close and tight.

47
00:02:13,322 --> 00:02:17,808
When a base pulls the hydrogen away,
the electron pair stays behind on

48
00:02:17,808 --> 00:02:18,739
the carbon.

49
00:02:18,739 --> 00:02:19,985
Watch the arrow.

50
00:02:20,385 --> 00:02:21,546
Pause and think.

51
00:02:21,546 --> 00:02:25,455
Whose hydrogen leaves most
easily: ethane, ethene, or ethyne?

52
00:02:28,655 --> 00:02:29,555
Ethyne!

53
00:02:29,275 --> 00:02:33,829
Its s p carbon has the most s character,
so it holds the electrons best.

54
00:02:33,829 --> 00:02:35,895
Ethene comes next, then ethane.

55
00:02:36,545 --> 00:02:38,428
Now, how do we make ethyne?

56
00:02:38,428 --> 00:02:39,799
One link at a time.

57
00:02:40,149 --> 00:02:41,049
Link one.

58
00:02:40,978 --> 00:02:43,296
Heat limestone, calcium carbonate.

59
00:02:43,296 --> 00:02:45,836
It gives quicklime and carbon dioxide.

60
00:02:46,586 --> 00:02:47,486
Link two.

61
00:02:47,401 --> 00:02:50,402
Heat quicklime with
coke, almost pure carbon.

62
00:02:50,402 --> 00:02:53,204
We get calcium carbide
and carbon monoxide.

63
00:02:53,954 --> 00:02:54,856
Link three.

64
00:02:54,856 --> 00:02:56,817
Add water to calcium carbide.

65
00:02:56,817 --> 00:02:58,170
Out bubbles ethyne.

66
00:02:58,170 --> 00:03:00,601
This is the stone in the mango box.

67
00:03:01,351 --> 00:03:02,251
Link four.

68
00:03:02,171 --> 00:03:06,315
Start from a vicinal dihalide, with
halogens on neighbouring carbons.

69
00:03:06,315 --> 00:03:08,661
Alcoholic KOH pulls off one HX.

70
00:03:09,411 --> 00:03:10,311
Link five.

71
00:03:10,162 --> 00:03:12,210
Sodamide pulls off the second HX.

72
00:03:12,210 --> 00:03:13,947
Now we have the triple bond.

73
00:03:14,697 --> 00:03:15,751
Remember this.

74
00:03:15,751 --> 00:03:19,503
One sigma, two pi, and an
end hydrogen that can leave.

75
00:03:20,203 --> 00:03:21,812
Now, one worked example.

76
00:03:21,812 --> 00:03:25,939
What does propyne give with sodium,
and with two molecules of HB r?

77
00:03:26,239 --> 00:03:27,139
Step one.

78
00:03:26,995 --> 00:03:30,223
Propyne has a hydrogen on
its end triple bond carbon.

79
00:03:30,223 --> 00:03:33,442
So sodium replaces it, and
hydrogen gas bubbles out.

80
00:03:34,192 --> 00:03:35,092
Step two.

81
00:03:34,981 --> 00:03:39,534
Pi bonds are weaker than sigma bonds,
so alkynes add things across them.

82
00:03:40,284 --> 00:03:41,184
Step three.

83
00:03:41,058 --> 00:03:43,490
The first HB r adds by Markovnikov's rule.

84
00:03:43,490 --> 00:03:46,729
Hydrogen goes to the carbon
that already has more hydrogens.

85
00:03:47,479 --> 00:03:48,379
Step four.

86
00:03:48,188 --> 00:03:50,280
So bromine lands on the middle carbon.

87
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:51,656
We get two bromopropene.

88
00:03:52,406 --> 00:03:53,306
Step five.

89
00:03:53,142 --> 00:03:55,292
The second HB r adds the same way.

90
00:03:55,292 --> 00:03:56,629
Two two dibromopropane.

91
00:03:57,279 --> 00:03:59,141
Alkynes add other things too.

92
00:03:59,541 --> 00:04:04,353
Hydrogen with a platinum, palladium
or nickel catalyst turns ethyne into

93
00:04:04,353 --> 00:04:06,023
ethene, then ethane.

94
00:04:06,423 --> 00:04:10,007
Reddish orange bromine in carbon
tetrachloride loses its colour.

95
00:04:10,007 --> 00:04:11,992
That is a test for unsaturation.

96
00:04:12,392 --> 00:04:15,937
Water adds with mercury ions and acid
at three hundred and thirty three

97
00:04:15,937 --> 00:04:16,837
kelvin.

98
00:04:16,471 --> 00:04:17,871
Propyne becomes propanone.

99
00:04:18,271 --> 00:04:22,529
Finally, pass ethyne through a red hot
iron tube at eight hundred and seventy

100
00:04:22,529 --> 00:04:23,429
three kelvin.

101
00:04:23,426 --> 00:04:25,818
Three molecules join into a benzene ring.

102
00:04:26,468 --> 00:04:27,930
Now, the exam trap.

103
00:04:28,280 --> 00:04:32,199
Students say every alkyne reacts with
sodium and gives hydrogen gas, so

104
00:04:32,199 --> 00:04:33,618
but two yne must too.

105
00:04:34,018 --> 00:04:34,918
Wrong.

106
00:04:34,615 --> 00:04:38,540
Only terminal alkynes have a
hydrogen on a triple bond carbon.

107
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:41,711
But two yne has none
there, so nothing happens.

108
00:04:42,361 --> 00:04:43,648
Where is this asked?

109
00:04:43,648 --> 00:04:46,956
Acidic alkynes, Markovnikov
addition and the carbide method.

110
00:04:47,556 --> 00:04:48,837
Exam level now.

111
00:04:48,837 --> 00:04:53,243
Compound X, C three H four,
gives hydrogen gas with sodium.

112
00:04:53,243 --> 00:04:56,814
With water, mercury ions
and acid, it gives Y.

113
00:04:56,814 --> 00:04:57,824
What is Y?

114
00:04:57,824 --> 00:04:59,159
Pause and solve.

115
00:05:04,559 --> 00:05:05,636
The answer is B.

116
00:05:05,636 --> 00:05:06,536
Propanone.

117
00:05:06,812 --> 00:05:10,896
X gives hydrogen with sodium,
so it is a terminal alkyne.

118
00:05:10,896 --> 00:05:12,917
C three H four is propyne.

119
00:05:13,417 --> 00:05:15,243
Water adds by Markovnikov's rule.

120
00:05:15,243 --> 00:05:18,318
The OH lands on the middle
carbon and forms an enol.

121
00:05:18,818 --> 00:05:20,882
The enol shifts into a ketone, propanone.

122
00:05:20,882 --> 00:05:23,188
Propanal would need the
OH on the end carbon.

123
00:05:23,788 --> 00:05:25,328
Here is your recap.

124
00:05:25,328 --> 00:05:26,911
Screenshot this card.

125
00:05:30,111 --> 00:05:32,297
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

126
00:05:32,697 --> 00:05:33,597
One.

127
00:05:33,174 --> 00:05:37,091
How many sigma and pi bonds are
in a carbon carbon triple bond?

128
00:05:37,891 --> 00:05:38,791
Two.

129
00:05:38,360 --> 00:05:42,525
Why is the hydrogen of ethyne more
acidic than the hydrogen of ethane?

130
00:05:43,325 --> 00:05:44,225
Three.

131
00:05:43,867 --> 00:05:46,418
What forms when calcium
carbide meets water?

132
00:05:47,318 --> 00:05:52,413
Next time: why does benzene refuse to
behave like it has three double bonds?

133
00:05:52,413 --> 00:05:55,088
We find out in benzene and aromaticity.
